<h1>The free tier and capacity maths</h1>
<p class="lede">What Cloudflare gives for free, which limit this app hits first, how many users fit, and what changes on the paid plan. Figures checked against Cloudflare's documentation in November 2026; check them again before relying on them.</p>

<h2>The free allowances</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Product</th><th>Free limit</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Worker requests</td><td>100,000 per day (static assets free and unlimited)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Worker CPU</td><td>10 ms per invocation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Subrequests</td><td>50 per invocation (D1 included)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cron triggers</td><td>5 per account</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D1 rows read</td><td>5,000,000 per day</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D1 rows written</td><td>100,000 per day</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D1 storage</td><td>500 MB per database, 5 GB total, 10 databases</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D1 queries</td><td>50 per invocation</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Sources: <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/">Workers limits</a>, <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/limits/">D1 limits</a>, <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/platform/pricing/">D1 pricing</a>.</p>

<h2>What happens at a limit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The free plan <strong>cannot charge you</strong>. Nothing upgrades automatically.</li>
  <li>Daily request limit: error 1027 (or 429 for paths that always run the Worker) until 00:00 UTC.</li>
  <li>CPU: error 1102 "Worker exceeded resource limits" for that invocation (occasional small overruns are tolerated).</li>
  <li>D1 daily limits: queries return errors until the reset.</li>
  <li>The monitor treats all of these as temporary failures: back off, keep the checkpoint, retry.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Counting one idle user check</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Cost</th><th>Amount</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Subrequests</td><td>1 (the incremental search)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>CPU</td><td>~0.4 ms (parse a snapshot of 300 bugs and 3000 seen ids)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D1 writes</td><td>0 (one heartbeat row every 20 minutes)</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>A user with one change and one device</h3>
<p>Search 1 + changelog 1 + comments 1 + commit 1 + devices 1 + push 1 + mark delivered 1 = <strong>7 subrequests</strong>; about 1.5 ms CPU; 2–3 rows written.</p>

<h2>Which limit comes first?</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Limit</th><th>Users per minute it allows</th><th>Binding?</th></tr>
  <tr><td>50 subrequests</td><td>~40 idle users (minus overhead)</td><td></td></tr>
  <tr><td>10 ms CPU</td><td>~12 users with a safety margin (some with changes)</td><td><strong>yes</strong></td></tr>
  <tr><td>100,000 writes/day</td><td>~700–1000 users in total</td><td></td></tr>
  <tr><td>100,000 requests/day</td><td>cron uses 1,440; leaves ~98,000 for API calls</td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<p>So runs are capped at <code>MONITOR_MAX_USERS_PER_RUN = 12</code>.</p>

<h2>Capacity formula</h2>
<pre><code>users without delay  ≈  users_per_run × interval_minutes
                     =  12 × 5   =  60  (default)
                     =  12 × 10  = 120
                     =  12 × 15  = 180</code></pre>
<p>Beyond that, users are deferred: they are checked a minute or two later than their slot. With 120 users at a 5-minute interval, the effective interval becomes about 10 minutes. Nothing is lost; it is just slower.</p>

<h2>Daily API requests</h2>
<p>A user opening the app makes roughly 5–10 Worker requests (config, status, events, mine, notify, a detail or two). 98,000 / 10 ≈ 9,800 app opens per day before the request limit matters.</p>

<h2>Upgrading (only if you ever need it)</h2>
<p>Workers Paid is a monthly subscription (check Cloudflare's current price). It raises CPU to 30 seconds per invocation by default, subrequests to 10,000, and D1 to billions of reads and tens of millions of writes per month included. For this app, raise <code>MONITOR_MAX_USERS_PER_RUN</code> (and optionally <code>MONITOR_SUBREQUEST_BUDGET</code>) after upgrading; nothing else changes.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-title">Never upgrade by accident</span>Upgrading is a deliberate action in the Cloudflare dashboard. Neither Wrangler nor this app does it. If you do upgrade, set a spending limit in the dashboard.</div>

<h2>Watching usage</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Dashboard → Workers &amp; Pages → your Worker → <strong>Metrics</strong>: requests, errors, CPU time (median and p99).</li>
  <li>Dashboard → D1 → your database → <strong>Metrics</strong>: rows read and written per day.</li>
  <li>Each cron log line shows the subrequests used (<code>"subrequests": 5</code>).</li>
</ul>
